# More Information

There's a lot more you can do when you're making courses with Markua. Here's a brief list of what else you can do:

* Assign a specific number of points to each question
* Make written or "fill in the blank" answers case-sensitive
* Specify the maximum number of lines or pages for an answer
* Assign more than one correct answer to a question
* Set it so the possible answers to a multiple choice question are presented in a random order to learners

# How to Write on Leanpub

This is a paragraph. You just write.

Blank lines are used to separate paragraphs.

To make *italic text* you surround it with *single* asterisks. To make **bold text** you surround it with **double** asterisks.

## Previewing and publishing

You can preview or publish your course by clicking a button!

To preview your course:

1. Click Useful Links on the left
2. Click Preview Page
3. Click the Create Preview button

To publish your course:

* Click Useful Links on the left
* Click Publish Page
* Click the Publish Your Course button

Yes, these instructions are not just help text: they are also a course manuscript which you can preview or even publish!

Once the course has been generated, you can view the latest version online to see what it looks like.

**In fact, we've already created a preview of your brand new course for you, just now!**

Just click on the Useful Links on the left, and click the Preview Page link to see it!

(If you get to the preview page and there are no course links yet, congratulations: you're a really fast reader! In that case, just wait a minute and then reload the page to see them.)

*Or, if you don't believe course publishing could possibly be this easy, just edit some of the text on this page and create your own preview from the Preview page by clicking the Create Preview button.*

## Basic formatting

You can start new lessons by starting a line with a # sign and a space, and then typing your lesson title, just like you can see at the top of this page, where you see # The Basics on a line by itself.

You can create multiple lessons in one file, but we recommend one lesson per file. This way, your manuscript files will be easier to navigate. So, for example, this lesson is contained in the file named "The Basics" that you can see listed under the "Manuscript" menu to the left. "The Basics" is in bold in the menu, because it is the file that is currently selected.

(If you start typing in here, the Manuscript menu and all the other menus will go away, so that you can relax and focus on your writing! Just click outside of this text area, and all the menus will come back.)

You can make a thematic break with three asterisks, like this:

* * *

By the way, the above formatting is actually *all* you need to know to write a non-technical course using Markdown formatting! To write a technical course, however, you'll need to know a bit more Markdown, and learn a bit about Markua.

## Markdown and Markua

The dialect of Markdown used at Leanpub is called Markua.

Markdown is great, but it doesn't have support for certain things that many courses need. These include index entries, crosslinks, endnotes, external code samples, etc. So, we've added these to Markdown, by extending Markdown in an open specification called Markua.

The Markua spec is [here](http://markua.com).

However, that spec is really long, and there are a few advanced things in the spec which aren't fully supported in Leanpub yet.

So, if you want to learn how to get started writing in Markua on Leanpub, see the next lesson, **Writing in Markua**, by clicking the Writing in Markua lesson under the Manuscript menu to the left.

This lesson will show you everything you need to know about how to use all the important parts of Markdown and Markua to create a technical course, including how to use...

   * lists
   * images
   * code samples
   * tables
   * math
   * resource attributes
   * document settings
   * asides and blurbs
   * crosslinks

Seriously, unless you already know everything about Markua, you should strongly consider looking at that lesson. It may save you a bunch of time!

